---
import DocShell from '../layouts/doc-shell.astro';
import { docsLinks } from '../lib/nav';
---

<DocShell
   title="MCP usage"
   summary="The MCP server is not a separate product. It is the same runtime the CLI uses, exposed for editors and agents that need tool calls and read-only resources."
   links={docsLinks}
>
   <section class="split panel">
      <div class="stack">
         <div class="panel-head">
            <p class="panel-kicker">Mental model</p>
            <h2>What MCP exposes</h2>
         </div>
      <p>
         The server exposes read-only standards resources plus tool calls for lookup, inspection,
         driver actions, execution, and verification.
      </p>
      </div>
      <div class="split-aside">
         <div class="tag">same runtime</div>
         <p class="aside-note">
            If the CLI command exists, the MCP server is usually wrapping the same underlying runtime
            path instead of inventing a second implementation.
         </p>
      </div>
   </section>

   <section class="ledger">
      <div class="panel-head">
         <p class="panel-kicker">Tool families</p>
         <h2>What agents can actually call</h2>
      </div>
      <div class="route-list">
         <div class="route-row"><strong>Knowledge</strong><span><code>doctor</code>, <code>criterion_lookup</code>, <code>level_lookup</code>, <code>search</code>, <code>coverage_lookup</code></span></div>
         <div class="route-row"><strong>Inspect</strong><span><code>inspect_applicable</code>, <code>inspect_criterion</code></span></div>
         <div class="route-row"><strong>Driver</strong><span><code>driver_start_session</code>, <code>driver_get_session</code>, <code>driver_stop_session</code>, <code>driver_next_item</code>, <code>driver_previous_item</code>, <code>driver_interact</code>, <code>driver_stop_interacting</code>, <code>driver_click_current_item</code>, <code>driver_read</code>, <code>driver_logs</code>, <code>driver_clear_logs</code>, <code>driver_key</code>, <code>driver_type</code>, <code>driver_checkpoint</code></span></div>
         <div class="route-row"><strong>Execution and verification</strong><span><code>run_axe</code>, <code>run_pattern</code>, <code>verify_criterion</code>, <code>verify_level</code></span></div>
      </div>
   </section>

   <section class="panel">
      <div class="panel-head">
         <p class="panel-kicker">Read-only surfaces</p>
         <h2>Resource uris</h2>
      </div>
      <ul>
         <li><code>a11ied://targets</code></li>
         <li><code>a11ied://wcag/criteria/&lt;version&gt;</code></li>
         <li><code>a11ied://wcag/levels/&lt;version&gt;</code></li>
         <li><code>a11ied://wcag/coverage/&lt;version&gt;</code></li>
         <li><code>a11ied://wcag/verification-strategies/&lt;version&gt;</code></li>
      </ul>
   </section>

   <section>
      <div class="panel-head">
         <p class="panel-kicker">Why this split exists</p>
         <h2>Read-only versus side-effecting operations</h2>
      </div>
      <p>
         Some questions are read-only standards lookups. Some launch browsers or drive assistive
         technology. The MCP server keeps that split visible so an agent can choose the small, cheap
         operation before it reaches for the expensive one.
      </p>
   </section>

   <section class="field-note">
      <div class="panel-head">
         <p class="panel-kicker">Agent rule</p>
         <h2>Do not hide side effects</h2>
      </div>
      <p>
         The active MCP tools can launch browsers, attach to targets, and drive VoiceOver or NVDA. That
         is real machine behavior, not just data retrieval. Agents need to treat those tools like
         execution surfaces, because that is what they are.
      </p>
   </section>
</DocShell>
